															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/a-random-day" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1841781); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1841781&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606162363" alt="A Random Day" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1841781)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/a-random-day" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1841781); return false;" >A Random Day</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606160599 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Nov 2020 19:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4826435">
									

<p>I'm deeply frustrated that the entire article ends up being the backstory for a one-off esoteric object class that <a href="/scp-1055">SCP-1055</a> proves can be handled just fine by Keter. The voting protocol is a fantastic way to open the article, the containment procedures prime me for a potentially interesting spin on antimemetics, and the O5 Council simply not existing at all is a hell of a hook&#8230; and then nothing is done with that hook.</p>
<p>None of the O5 Council's spooky missives have weight because they aren't being associated with any actions of consequence. The letters from each administrator to their successor similarly lack weight because we don't know what kind of evil each one was complicit in or what regrets they have in complying with the Council. The Pripyat Incident might be Chernobyl but I don't know because there's no context for any of these actions or decisions. The O5 Meeting Minutes are similarly inconsequential because it's just vaguely spooky sounding dialogue that doesn't build up to anything.</p>
<p>The O5 correspondence with Michelle is the most promising part of this article. Despite the complete lack of buildup to this point, I still felt some catharsis when she defied them and passed the test. And then that catharsis was deflated utterly when she used her newfound freedom and authority to&#8230; create and define a new one-off object class.</p>
<p>The article clearly wants to be a meditation on the struggle of holding to one's ideals and morals in the face of authoritarian and institutional pressure, but it doesn't demonstrate that challenge effectively. Show us what crises the previous Administrators faced and how the Council ground them down. Show us how powerful the idea of the Council is and demonstrate Michelle's moral fortitude through more than just back-and-forth emails. I understand that the object class is supposed to be used to justify why there's so little documentation and context for this article, but it would be just as easy to say that the &quot;Atelis Protocol&quot; mandates these infosecurity requirements without wasting so much time reminding us that this thing has an esoteric object class. Forget the esoteric object class and focus on the power struggle.</p>

							</div>

								
