					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vincent-van-gone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4874299); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4874299&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606162363" alt="Vincent Van Gone" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4874299)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/vincent-van-gone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4874299); return false;" >Vincent Van Gone</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606160939 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Nov 2020 19:48</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4826438">
									

<p>The main reason I downvoted was because I didn't really like the portrayal of the O5 Council here and I had some issues with pacing. I think everything before the addenda is perfect, especially given how the absence of the O5 Council in the Containment Procedures looks right after reading the description. I also think the interactions between the O5 Council and the Administrator are the strongest part of the article, especially given the crosslink to your tale.</p>
<p>I had issues with the lack of coherence regarding the O5 Council, which I know is the point of the article. There might be something I missed, but I don't see the point of having Addendum 2 and Addendum 4. To me, they really served the same purpose of establishing the Council as an entity which makes cryptic statements. That's a bit of a reductive summary of that, but it feels a bit redundant to have both of them. I do believe having one of them is essential though to provide characterization for them before their interaction with the Administrator.</p>
<p>Additionally, I felt that Addendum 6 did not contribute a lot to the article. I felt the reiteration of what the object class meant was unnecessary. I feel like the message from the Administrator at the end is important, although I feel that the object class should have received no more than a throwaway mention (something like acknowledging the difficulty of classifying this anomaly, but nothing else).</p>

							</div>

								
					
